{"name":"Wolverine","hex":"#91989d","fetched":"2026-10-13","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"Wolverine","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A native or resident of the American state of Michigan.","Someone associated with the University of Michigan, for example as a student, alum, or sports team member, or as a fan."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/Wolverine"}],"entries":[{"title":"Wolverine","blocks":[{"text":"The wolverine, also called the carcajou or quickhatch, is the largest land-dwelling member of the family Mustelidae. It is a muscular carnivore and a solitary animal. The wolverine has a reputation for ferocity and strength out of proportion to its size, with the documented ability to kill prey many times larger than itself."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/86/J%C3%A4rv1.jpg/330px-J%C3%A4rv1.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wolverine"},{"title":"Wolverine (character)","blocks":[{"text":"Wolverine is a superhero appearing in American comic books published by Marvel Comics. The character first appeared in the comic book The Incredible Hulk #180 (1974) and is best known as a member of the superhero team, the X-Men. Wolverine is the alias of James \"Logan\" Howlett, a mutant born in Canada in the late 19th century. He possesses a range of superpowers including highly advanced self-healing abilities, a significantly prolonged lifespan, animal-keen senses, and retractable claws. His skeleton is reinforced with the unbreakable fictional metal adamantium, which he acquired after becoming an unwilling test subject in the Weapon X super soldier program. Wolverine is commonly depicted as a gruff loner susceptible to animalistic \"berserker rages\" who struggles to reconcile his humanity with his wild nature."}],"image":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/d/d3/Wolverine_%28circa_2024%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wolverine_(character)"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/89/Gulo_gulo.jpg/500px-Gulo_gulo.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/89/Gulo_gulo.jpg/1280px-Gulo_gulo.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/89/Gulo_gulo.jpg/500px-Gulo_gulo.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Wolverine skull from the Pleistocene of Germany at the Natural History Museum, Berlin","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AGulo_gulo.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c6/Gulo_gulo_-_Museo_Civico_di_Storia_Naturale_Giacomo_Doria_-_Genoa%2C_Italy_-_DSC02641.JPG/500px-Gulo_gulo_-_Museo_Civico_di_Storia_Naturale_Giacomo_Doria_-_Genoa%2C_Italy_-_DSC02641.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c6/Gulo_gulo_-_Museo_Civico_di_Storia_Naturale_Giacomo_Doria_-_Genoa%2C_Italy_-_DSC02641.JPG/1280px-Gulo_gulo_-_Museo_Civico_di_Storia_Naturale_Giacomo_Doria_-_Genoa%2C_Italy_-_DSC02641.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c6/Gulo_gulo_-_Museo_Civico_di_Storia_Naturale_Giacomo_Doria_-_Genoa%2C_Italy_-_DSC02641.JPG/500px-Gulo_gulo_-_Museo_Civico_di_Storia_Naturale_Giacomo_Doria_-_Genoa%2C_Italy_-_DSC02641.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Stuffed individual at the Museo Civico di Storia Naturale Giacomo Doria, Italy","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AGulo_gulo_-_Museo_Civico_di_Storia_Naturale_Giacomo_Doria_-_Genoa%2C_Italy_-_DSC02641.JPG"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimed